 Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Before admitting a completed sheet, evict enough existing sheets to remain within that bound. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
 
-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+## Eviction Policy: 2Q
+
+The cache uses 2Q, which separates first-use entries from reused entries so a long sequential screening cannot evict the comparison working set. All policy state — the two queues and their byte accounting — is private to this worker.
+
+Two queues hold resident entries:
+
+- **A1 (first-use queue)** — FIFO order. Entries admitted after a cache miss enter here at the head.
+- **Am (reused queue)** — LRU order. Entries that are requested a second time move here at the most-recently-used end; subsequent hits also refresh them to that end.
+
+The 12 GiB budget is split between the queues: A1 may hold at most 3 GiB and Am at most 9 GiB. Both are tunable parameters of this worker; the 3/9 split is the starting value and may be adjusted after observing screening workloads.
+
+Replacement rules (eviction always precedes admission):
+
+- Before admitting a newly rendered sheet: while admitting it would push A1 over its 3 GiB split, evict the FIFO tail of A1; after that, if admitting it would still push total cached JPEG data over budget, evict the LRU tail of Am.
+- Before promoting an A1 entry into Am: if Am is at its 9 GiB split, evict the LRU tail of Am.
+- "Exceeds" means strictly greater than the split; filling a queue exactly to its split is allowed.
+- Eviction only targets resident sheets; it never touches pending render jobs, job tickets, or source footage. Eviction done for an admission is not rolled back if that render later fails.
+
+A sheet evicted from A1 that is requested again has no memory of its first stay (there is no ghost queue); it is simply admitted to A1 as a first-use entry. This trades a small amount of scan resistance for avoiding fingerprint bookkeeping.
+
+Only requests answered with the cached JPEG count as reuse; a request that received a job ticket for an in-flight render does not move the entry between queues.
+
+Because renderer versions are part of the cache key, sheets rendered under a superseded renderer version have no requesters. They are not specially treated: 2Q evicts them by the same rules as any other unreused entry.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Cache eviction policy | 2Q with a 3/9 GiB A1/Am byte split | LRU; frequency-based eviction with aging | Long screenings evict the comparison working set under pure LRU; aged frequency counters add maintenance and lag project switches. 2Q keeps reused sheets resident across one-off scans for two queues' worth of bookkeeping, confined to this worker. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Deferred
+1. The A1/Am byte split starts at 3/9 GiB and may need retuning once real screening workloads are observed; the exact values are worker configuration, not interface.
